<HelpTab>
    <FluentText>This tab is for Admins and people with the CanManageFiles role.</FluentText>

    <HelpSection Title="Uploading">
        <FluentText>
            Select <strong>Upload</strong> to choose files, or drag them from your computer and drop them anywhere on
            Files. They go into the folder that's open, or drop them onto a folder (a tile or a folder in the tree) to
            put them straight in there. You can drop a whole folder too: its folders are made in Files for you.
        </FluentText>
        <FluentText>
            To add a screenshot or a copied picture, press <strong>Ctrl+V</strong> (<strong>⌘V</strong> on a Mac)
            while Files is open. On a phone, <strong>Upload</strong> also lets you take a photo.
        </FluentText>
        <FluentText>
            Each file shows in the upload box at the bottom right with its progress. You can cancel one, try a failed
            one again, or jump to the folder it went into. Keep the page open until they're done: Lanyard warns you if
            you try to leave.
        </FluentText>
        <FluentText>
            Videos can be up to 500 MB and other files up to 100 MB. You can upload images (PNG, JPG, GIF, WebP, BMP,
            SVG, HEIC), video (MP4, M4V, WebM, MOV, MKV, AVI), audio (MP3, WAV, FLAC, OGG, AAC, M4A, WMA), PDFs, Word,
            Excel and PowerPoint files and their OpenDocument versions, RTF, text, CSV, JSON and ZIP files. Other types,
            such as programs or web pages, are refused. Audio files are also added to the music library.
        </FluentText>
        <FluentText>
            Two files in the same folder can't have the same name. If you upload one that's already there, the new
            copy gets a number, like <strong>poster (2).png</strong>, and the upload box says so.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Folders">
        <FluentText>
            <strong>New folder</strong> makes a folder inside the one that's open. Folder names must be different from
            the other folders beside them.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Renaming, moving and deleting">
        <FluentText>
            Every file and folder has a <strong>⋯</strong> button (or right-click it) with <strong>Rename</strong>,
            <strong>Move to…</strong> and <strong>Delete</strong>. The same buttons are in its details. Renaming a
            file keeps its type: you change the name before the extension.
        </FluentText>
        <FluentText>
            <strong>Move to…</strong> shows your folders. Pick one and select <strong>Move here</strong>. A folder
            can't be moved into itself or one of its own folders.
        </FluentText>
        <FluentText>
            With a file or folder selected, <strong>F2</strong> renames it, <strong>Delete</strong> deletes it and
            <strong>Space</strong> previews a file.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Moving by dragging">
        <FluentText>
            Drag a file or folder onto another folder to move it there: a folder tile, a folder in the tree on the
            left, or a folder in the path at the top. If several are selected, dragging one of them moves them all.
            The <strong>Move</strong> button in the selection bar does the same without dragging.
        </FluentText>
        <FluentText>
            After a move, or after deleting, a bar at the bottom offers <strong>Undo</strong> for a few seconds. It
            puts everything back where it was.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="What uses a file">
        <FluentText>
            A file's details list everything in Lanyard that uses it under <strong>Used by</strong>: the company logo,
            a projection program, a training course, party printout artwork, an onboarding email or a song in the
            music library. Files that are used somewhere have a link badge in the grid.
        </FluentText>
        <HelpNote Intent="MessageBarIntent.Info">
            Deleting a file that's in use asks you first. It keeps working where it's used while it's in the
            Recycle bin, and it can't be deleted for good until nothing uses it.
        </HelpNote>
    </HelpSection>
</HelpTab>
